# dsv41 round: the CUDA graph cache keeps at most GRAPH_CAP stretches (issue #69). round.Graphs captured a stretch the
# first time its (rows, context bucket, stretch, shard mode) came up and kept every graph until the process ended: the
# keys add up over days as conversations grow past each power of two (16 rows x 12 buckets at POOL=2097152 x 3
# stretches), each graph holding device memory and about 1.6 times as much host memory on both ranks, until memguard
# killed rank 0 after two days of agent traffic.
# - graph_cache.zig: a map with a cap on its entries; past the cap a put hands back the least recently used entry (a
#   replay counts as a use) for its owner to free. Host tests: the eviction order, the cap held over 600 new keys,
#   cap 0 keeps all.
# - round.Graphs keeps its graphs there and destroys an evicted one with cuGraphExecDestroy (a graph still running is
#   freed when it completes); the stretch is captured again when its key comes back. Both ranks see the same keys in
#   the same order, so with the same cap they keep and drop the same graphs. A new graph is launched before it is
#   kept: before, a failed first launch freed a graph the map still held. The log says when the cache reaches 64 and
#   128 graphs and, past the cap, at each power of two of evictions.
# - draft.PassGraphs is unchanged: its key (streams, steps) is bounded by --parallel and the draft block.
# Switch: TF_DS_GRAPH_CAP (rank 0) and --graph-cap (rank 1), the same number on both; 0 or unset keeps every graph as
# before. Recipe setting GRAPH_CAP, default 128 (start.sh sends --graph-cap only with GRAPHS=1).
# Measured on the pair (engine 629c524 with this recipe's 0001-0008, 0010 and the EAQ v1.1 float linears; 4 streams,
# drafts on, POOL=2097152): GRAPH_CAP=3 (a capture almost every stretch, 4096 evictions a rank) gives the same replies
# as GRAPHS=0, tools/exact.py IDENTICAL 31/31, with one stream about 5% slower than uncapped graphs (34.5 against
# 33.0 ms/chunk, median of 7). GRAPH_CAP=128 decodes as uncapped: one stream 33.0 / 33.0, four streams at ~60K 63.9 /
# 64.0 ms/chunk (mean of 8). A repeated sweep of 8 fresh conversations (500 to 32K words) then grows rank 0's GPU
# memory only by the prompt cache's kept states (+38 MiB a pass, as with GRAPHS=0), with rank 1's RSS flat (the sweeps
# ran before the launch was moved ahead of the put, which changes no GPU work).
